Q: NoSuchMethodException, No suitable constructor: migrating jenkins to jetty12 ee9 (jenkins 492.3) with an existing HashLoginService implementation A: <p>I ran into the same issue migrating to Jetty 12. The problem boils down to one thing: <code>HashLoginService.setConfig()</code> no longer accepts a <code>String</code> — it now requires a <code>Resource</code> object. And constructing a <code>Resource</code> in Jetty XML isn't obvious because the docs haven't caught up.</p>
<p>The trick is to use <code>ResourceFactory.root().newResource(path)</code> expressed in Jetty XML's static call syntax:</p>
<p><strong>Working <code>jetty.xml</code> config (server-level bean):</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;!DOCTYPE Configure PUBLIC &quot;-//Jetty//Configure//EN&quot; &quot;https://jetty.org/configure_10_0.dtd&quot;&gt;

&lt;Configure id=&quot;Server&quot; class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.server.Server&quot;&gt;
  &lt;Call name=&quot;addBean&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;
      &lt;New class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.security.HashLoginService&quot;&gt;
        &lt;Set name=&quot;name&quot;&gt;Jenkins Realm&lt;/Set&gt;
        &lt;Set name=&quot;config&quot;&gt;
          &lt;Call class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.util.resource.ResourceFactory&quot; name=&quot;root&quot;&gt;
            &lt;Call name=&quot;newResource&quot;&gt;
              &lt;Arg&gt;&lt;SystemProperty name=&quot;jetty.base&quot; default=&quot;.&quot;/&gt;/resources/realm.properties&lt;/Arg&gt;
            &lt;/Call&gt;
          &lt;/Call&gt;
        &lt;/Set&gt;
      &lt;/New&gt;
    &lt;/Arg&gt;
  &lt;/Call&gt;
&lt;/Configure&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>What's happening: <code>&lt;Call class=&quot;...&quot; name=&quot;root&quot;&gt;</code> invokes the static <code>ResourceFactory.root()</code>, and then the nested <code>&lt;Call name=&quot;newResource&quot;&gt;</code> chains on the returned factory. The <code>newResource(String)</code> overload handles plain file paths fine — no need to mess with <code>java.net.URI</code> or <code>PathResource</code> constructors.</p>
<p>If you want the resource lifecycle tied to the Server instead of the JVM, swap <code>root</code> for <code>of</code> and pass the Server ref:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Set name=&quot;config&quot;&gt;
  &lt;Call class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.util.resource.ResourceFactory&quot; name=&quot;of&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Arg&gt;&lt;Ref refid=&quot;Server&quot;/&gt;&lt;/Arg&gt;
    &lt;Call name=&quot;newResource&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Arg&gt;&lt;SystemProperty name=&quot;jetty.base&quot; default=&quot;.&quot;/&gt;/resources/realm.properties&lt;/Arg&gt;
    &lt;/Call&gt;
  &lt;/Call&gt;
&lt;/Set&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Registering as a server bean like this means any webapp with a matching <code>&lt;realm-name&gt;</code> in its <code>web.xml</code> will pick it up automatically — you don't need per-webapp context XML at all.</p>
<p>If you do need it scoped to just the Jenkins webapp (context XML), the other gotcha is that <code>&lt;Get name=&quot;securityHandler&quot;&gt;</code> doesn't work on the ee9 <code>WebAppContext</code> through the XML config engine anymore. You have to <code>&lt;Set&gt;</code> the whole security handler instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;Configure class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.ee9.webapp.WebAppContext&quot;&gt;
  &lt;Set name=&quot;contextPath&quot;&gt;/jenkins&lt;/Set&gt;
  &lt;Set name=&quot;war&quot;&gt;&lt;Property name=&quot;jetty.webapps&quot; default=&quot;.&quot;/&gt;/jenkins.war&lt;/Set&gt;
  &lt;Set name=&quot;securityHandler&quot;&gt;
    &lt;New class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.ee9.security.ConstraintSecurityHandler&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Set name=&quot;loginService&quot;&gt;
        &lt;New class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.security.HashLoginService&quot;&gt;
          &lt;Set name=&quot;name&quot;&gt;Jenkins Realm&lt;/Set&gt;
          &lt;Set name=&quot;config&quot;&gt;
            &lt;Call class=&quot;org.eclipse.jetty.util.resource.ResourceFactory&quot; name=&quot;root&quot;&gt;
              &lt;Call name=&quot;newResource&quot;&gt;
                &lt;Arg&gt;&lt;SystemProperty name=&quot;jetty.base&quot; default=&quot;.&quot;/&gt;/resources/realm.properties&lt;/Arg&gt;
              &lt;/Call&gt;
            &lt;/Call&gt;
          &lt;/Set&gt;
        &lt;/New&gt;
      &lt;/Set&gt;
    &lt;/New&gt;
  &lt;/Set&gt;
&lt;/Configure&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Note: make sure you use <code>org.eclipse.jetty.ee9.security.ConstraintSecurityHandler</code> (the ee9 package), not the core one.</p>
<p>For context on why your other attempts failed — the two-arg constructor <code>HashLoginService(String, String)</code> is now <code>HashLoginService(String, Resource)</code> (<a href="https://javadoc.jetty.org/jetty-12/org/eclipse/jetty/security/HashLoginService.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">javadoc</a>), and your <code>ResourceFactory.of(Server)</code> attempt was close but the nested <code>&lt;Call&gt;</code> on <code>newResource</code> wasn't scoping correctly because the inner <code>&lt;Call&gt;</code> was being evaluated as a setter on <code>ResourceFactory</code> rather than chaining on the return value. Also the raw path you passed wasn't a valid URI, which is why you got <code>IllegalStateException: No Method</code>.</p>
<p>This is a known gap — <a href="https://github.com/jetty/jetty.project/issues/10068" rel="nofollow noreferrer">jetty/jetty.project#10068</a> tracks the original report about <code>Resource</code> not being easily instantiable from XML/Maven configs.</p>

Q: How to call MSYS2&#39;s git.exe from a Windows program or IDE? A: <p>The root cause is that MSYS2's git is Cygwin-based and expects paths like <code>/c/Users/foo</code>
instead of <code>C:\Users\foo</code>. The fix is to place a <strong>wrapper executable</strong> earlier in
the tool's configured git path, which:</p>
<ol>
<li>Translates all Windows-style path arguments before forwarding them to the real git</li>
<li>Translates key environment variables (<code>HOME</code>, <code>USERPROFILE</code>, <code>TEMP</code>) so git can
locate <code>~/.gitconfig</code> and <code>~/.ssh/</code></li>
<li>Prepends <code>C:\msys64\usr\bin</code> to <code>PATH</code> so git's runtime dependencies
(like <code>ssh</code>, <code>less</code>) are found</li>
<li>Sets <code>MSYSTEM=MSYS</code> so the MSYS2 runtime initializes correctly</li>
</ol>
<h3>Path Translation Rules</h3>
<div class="s-table-container"><table class="s-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Input (Windows)</th>
<th>Output (MSYS2)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>C:\Users\foo\repo</code></td>
<td><code>/c/Users/foo/repo</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>--work-tree=D:\project</code></td>
<td><code>--work-tree=/d/project</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>my docs\file.txt</code></td>
<td><code>my docs/file.txt</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>HOME=C:\Users\foo</code></td>
<td><code>HOME=/c/Users/foo</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h3>The Wrapper (Go)</h3>
<p>Save this as <code>git_wrapper.go</code>, configure the two constants at the top, then build:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>go build -o git.exe git_wrapper.go
</code></pre>
<p>Place the resulting <code>git.exe</code> somewhere that comes <strong>before</strong> <code>C:\msys64\usr\bin</code>
in your tool's git path setting.</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>package main

import (
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;os&quot;
    &quot;os/exec&quot;
    &quot;regexp&quot;
    &quot;strings&quot;
    &quot;syscall&quot;
)

const (
    RealGitPath = `C:\msys64\usr\bin\git.exe`
    MsysBinPath = `C:\msys64\usr\bin`
)

var winPathRegex   = regexp.MustCompile(`^(?i)([a-z]):[\\/](.*)$`)
var eqWinPathRegex = regexp.MustCompile(`^([^=]+=)(?i)([a-z]):[\\/](.*)$`)

func translateArg(arg string) string {
    if m := eqWinPathRegex.FindStringSubmatch(arg); m != nil {
        return fmt.Sprintf(&quot;%s/%s/%s&quot;, m, strings.ToLower(m),[16][17]
            strings.ReplaceAll(m, &quot;\\&quot;, &quot;/&quot;))[18]
    }
    if m := winPathRegex.FindStringSubmatch(arg); m != nil {
        return fmt.Sprintf(&quot;/%s/%s&quot;, strings.ToLower(m),[16]
            strings.ReplaceAll(m, &quot;\\&quot;, &quot;/&quot;))[17]
    }
    if strings.Contains(arg, &quot;\\&quot;) {
        arg = strings.ReplaceAll(arg, &quot;\\&quot;, &quot;/&quot;)
    }
    return arg
}

func translateEnvPath(value string) string {
    if m := winPathRegex.FindStringSubmatch(value); m != nil {
        return fmt.Sprintf(&quot;/%s/%s&quot;, strings.ToLower(m),[16]
            strings.ReplaceAll(m, &quot;\\&quot;, &quot;/&quot;))[17]
    }
    return value
}

func main() {
    selfPath, err := os.Executable()
    if err == nil &amp;&amp; strings.EqualFold(selfPath, RealGitPath) {
        fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, &quot;Fatal Error: Wrapper is pointing to itself.&quot;)
        os.Exit(1)
    }

    var args []string
    for _, arg := range os.Args[1:] {
        args = append(args, translateArg(arg))
    }

    cmd := exec.Command(RealGitPath, args...)
    cmd.Stdin, cmd.Stdout, cmd.Stderr = os.Stdin, os.Stdout, os.Stderr

    pathEnvKeys := map[string]bool{
        &quot;HOME&quot;: true, &quot;USERPROFILE&quot;: true,
        &quot;TEMP&quot;: true, &quot;TMP&quot;: true, &quot;TMPDIR&quot;: true,
    }

    var newEnv []string
    hasMsystem := false
    for _, e := range os.Environ() {
        upper := strings.ToUpper(e)
        if strings.HasPrefix(upper, &quot;PATH=&quot;) {
            newEnv = append(newEnv, &quot;PATH=&quot;+MsysBinPath+string(os.PathListSeparator)+e[5:])
            continue
        }
        if strings.HasPrefix(upper, &quot;MSYSTEM=&quot;) {
            hasMsystem = true
            newEnv = append(newEnv, e)
            continue
        }
        translated := false
        for key := range pathEnvKeys {
            if strings.HasPrefix(upper, key+&quot;=&quot;) {
                newEnv = append(newEnv, key+&quot;=&quot;+translateEnvPath(e[len(key)+1:]))
                translated = true
                break
            }
        }
        if !translated {
            newEnv = append(newEnv, e)
        }
    }
    if !hasMsystem {
        newEnv = append(newEnv, &quot;MSYSTEM=MSYS&quot;)
    }
    cmd.Env = newEnv

    if err = cmd.Run(); err != nil {
        if exitErr, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError); ok {
            if status, ok := exitErr.Sys().(syscall.WaitStatus); ok {
                os.Exit(status.ExitStatus())
            }
            os.Exit(exitErr.ExitCode())
        }
        fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, &quot;Git Wrapper Error: %v\n&quot;, err)
        os.Exit(1)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Notes</h3>
<ul>
<li>This wrapper is <strong>transparent</strong>: exit codes, stdin/stdout/stderr are all forwarded
unchanged, so the calling program behaves as if it called git directly.</li>
<li>The infinite-loop guard prevents accidental misconfiguration from causing a crash loop.</li>
<li>If you use a non-standard MSYS2 install path, update the two constants at the top.</li>
</ul>
